/*!
\page grammar_format The .lex grammar format

A `.lex` file is a lexer written as text: the input of the `scilex` CLI, of `scilex::parse_grammar` and
`scilex::load_grammar` (`<scilex/grammar.hpp>`, which `<scilex/scilex.hpp>` does not include), and of Python's
`scilex.parse_grammar` and `scilex.load_grammar`. It describes the same \ref scilex::rule lists a C++ program builds
in code, one rule per line. The format is **experimental** (`RELEASING.md`): a release may change it, and its notes
say how.

\tableofcontents

\section grammar_lines Lines

The text is UTF-8. A byte order mark that starts it is dropped. Lines end at `\n`; a `\r` before it is dropped
too. On each line:

- a line that is empty, all spaces and tabs, or whose first non-blank character is `#`, is ignored;
- the spaces and tabs that end a line are dropped, so no field ends in one (write a final space as `[ ]` or
  `\x20`);
- a line whose first non-blank character is `%` is a \ref grammar_directives "directive";
- any other line is a rule, its fields separated by **tabs**: a name, a tab, a pattern, and optionally a tab and
  the options. A line with one field, or with more than three, is refused.

\section grammar_syntax Syntax

\verbatim
grammar   = { line } ;
line      = blank | comment | directive | rule ;
blank     = { " " | TAB } ;
comment   = { " " | TAB } "#" { any-but-newline } ;
directive = { " " | TAB } "%insignificant" { " " | TAB }+ mode { "," mode } ;
rule      = { " " | TAB } name TAB pattern [ TAB options ] ;
name      = { any-but-tab-or-newline }+ ;
pattern   = { any-but-tab-or-newline }+ ;        (* REAL's syntax: real::regex(pattern) *)
options   = option { " "+ option } ;             (* spaces between options; none twice on a line *)
option    = "skip" | "pop" | "fragment" | "line_start" | "not_line_start"
          | "in=" mode { "," mode }
          | "push=" mode | "set=" mode
          | "after=" ref { "," ref } | "not_after=" ref { "," ref }
          | "indent=" ( "entry" | "key" | "value" | "reset" )
          | "extend=" ( "block" | "lines" )
          | "continue=" name ;
mode      = { any-but-comma-space-tab }+ ;
ref       = name | "^" ;
\endverbatim

\section grammar_meaning Meaning

- **Kinds.** A rule's kind is its 0-based position among the rules, fragments not counted;
  \ref scilex::grammar::names maps a kind back to its name. Names need not be unique among rules.
- **The pattern** is compiled as `real::regex(pattern)`, REAL's default flags: inline flags such as `(?i)` or
  `(?a)` (ASCII-pinned `\w`, `\d`, `\s`) set others. An invalid pattern is refused at its column.
- `skip`: the rule's matches are consumed and not emitted.
- `in=m1,m2`: the modes the rule is active in; without it, `default` alone.
- `push=m`, `set=m`, `pop`: the mode transition fired when the rule wins, at most one. A mode that `push=` or
  `set=` enters must have a rule active in it.
- `after=A,B` and `not_after=A,B`: the rule is active only right after (or except right after) a token of a rule
  named `A` or `B`, every rule of that name, earlier or later in the file; `^` stands for the start of the input
  (\ref scilex::rule::after). A line takes one of the two, once.
- `line_start` and `not_line_start`: the rule is active only at the start of a line, or only past it
  (\ref scilex::rule::at_line_start).
- `indent=` and `extend=`: the rule's part in the indentation the lexer tracks, and how its token extends below
  its line (Layout Awareness Level B, \ref rule_indentation).
- `continue=N`, with `extend=lines`: the pattern each continued line must match, the fragment named `N`, else the
  first rule of that name.
- `fragment`: the line names a pattern for `continue=` and is not a rule. It takes no other option, has no kind,
  is never lexed, and no other fragment shares its name.

\section grammar_directives Directives

The one directive is `%insignificant m1,m2`: the modes whose tokens carry no layout structure (the lexer's
`insignificant_modes`). Each mode named must have a rule active in it; the directive may repeat. Any other word
after `%` is refused.

\section grammar_errors Errors

The first malformed line stops the reading: no half-built grammar is returned. The error,
\ref scilex::grammar_error (Python: `scilex.GrammarError`), reads `origin:line:column: cause`, the form compilers
and editors recognize, with the 1-based line and byte column of the fault, or `origin: cause` for a fault with
no line (an unreadable file, a grammar with no rules). Its `line()`, `column()` and `cause()` give the parts.

\section grammar_example An example

A string with escapes, lexed in a mode of its own, beside numbers and skipped whitespace (the fields separated by
tabs):

\verbatim
WS	\s+	skip
%insignificant str
STRING	"	push=str
TEXT	[^"\\]+	in=str
ESCAPE	\\.	in=str
END	"	in=str pop
NUMBER	[0-9]+
\endverbatim
*/
